# Independent review: stored original recovery payload bridge

Verdict: no blocking finding in the reviewed bridge. Exact reviewed source snapshots are under source/; the implementation diff was reviewed against base bf9b26d.

Reviewed the release.recover addition, its tests, README, and the original storage/recovery validators against chapter 15's requirement to retain the original Pages tar bytes and verify the saved payload rather than rebuilding it. recover first validates release metadata, all asset pins and actual download bytes, selects payload.tar by validated name, and passes its pinned SHA-256 plus the separately supplied manifest digest into recovery.verify. It returns the storage receipt and original payload object; it neither extracts files nor substitutes newly serialized bytes. Storage failures short-circuit before a recoverable result can be returned.

Independent execution: six release tests and six recovery tests passed normally and with Python -O. The real saved 22-file tar retains object identity and its previously archived tar/manifest digests. Unknown tar bytes, mutable release metadata and wrong manifest pins are rejected. Additional independent probes reverse asset-pin and metadata order to verify name-based selection, then use compressed wrappers, an extra trailing zero block and a truncated archive with internally matching storage metadata/hashes. Storage validation passes those byte-consistent artifacts, but recover rejects each invalid archive. commands.json, probe-results.json and raw logs preserve the evidence.

The README accurately separates storage/tar verification from LKG authorization. Expected pins and the manifest digest must already be bound to a validated LKG record; identity/smoke JSON semantics, authenticated release/download transport, current publication reconciliation and deployment eligibility remain caller responsibilities. The tests do not establish any of those. No live network call, release publication, filesystem extraction, deployment or LKG promotion was performed by this reviewer. No implementation edits were made.
